# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; this template is not.
#
#   PowerShell:  $env:SIH26_HMAC_KEY = "..."     (this shell only)
#   bash:        export SIH26_HMAC_KEY="..."
#
# Neither key has a default. Both modules raise a RuntimeError naming the variable
# when it is unset, because a default key that ships in the source tree is a
# published key (F35/F36).

# --- SIH26_HMAC_KEY -- node identity anonymisation (data/anonymize.py) --------
#
# Salts the HMAC that turns an IP into a pseudonym, the per-epoch node-id
# permutation, and the /24 bucket. It is a REPRODUCIBILITY PARAMETER, not a
# secret: CIC-IDS-2018 is a public dataset, so the key protects nothing an
# attacker could not already look up. But it changes real model inputs -- a
# different key re-buckets every host into different /24 buckets and different
# per-epoch node ids, which changes the feature matrix, which makes every metric
# incomparable with one computed under another key (diagnosis_report.md, section
# 0: an unrecorded key is what invalidated an earlier benchmark table).
#
# THIS REPOSITORY DOES NOT CONTAIN THE VALUE our published table was measured
# under, and no file here can hand it to you. It lives only in the gitignored
# .env on the machine that produced that table; docs/INSTALL.md calls it the team
# key, never committed. So:
#   - To reproduce OUR published numbers: get that .env from the team. A value
#     you invent will not match, and the table will not reproduce.
#   - For anything else (a fresh training run, the smoke test, a demo): any
#     value works. Pick one, keep using it, and record it beside whatever
#     numbers you produce -- an unrecorded key is an unreproducible result.
SIH26_HMAC_KEY=set-a-value-and-record-it-with-your-results

# --- SIH26_LEDGER_KEY -- audit ledger (ledger/ledger.py) ---------------------
#
# Two jobs, and unlike the key above this one IS a secret:
#
# 1. Pseudonymises the `host` field of every ledger record. The dataset's
#    attackers are ~15 fixed public addresses, so anyone holding this key
#    reverses every pseudonym in the ledger with a 15-row rainbow table.
# 2. Signs each checkpoint (via a domain-separated derived key). The chain and
#    its checkpoint log live in the same run directory, so the signature is the
#    only thing a forger who owns that directory cannot reproduce. Verification
#    needs this key: `python -m ledger.verify_cli <chain>` cannot check an anchor
#    without it.
#
# Needed by everything that constructs a Ledger: engine/predict.py (so, any
# analysis run from the Streamlit app), app/panels.py's ledger card,
# scripts/edge_case_report.py, scripts/anchor_checkpoint.py and
# `python -m ledger.verify_cli`.
#
# That includes BOTH of CLAUDE.md's definition-of-done commands, which is the
# easy thing to miss:
#   - `python -m tests.smoke` goes straight through the ledger -- tests/smoke.py
#     calls engine.predict.predict_file (which constructs a Ledger and
#     checkpoints it) and then ledger.verify_cli.verify. It is NOT run under
#     pytest, so nothing hands it a key: it reads this environment or it raises.
#   - `pytest tests/ -q` needs one too, but supplies its own. The session
#     fixture in tests/conftest.py does `os.environ.setdefault`, so the suite is
#     green with this unset and keeps a real exported key when you have one.
#
# NOT needed by `python -m engine.train_engine`, which never touches the ledger
# -- training works with this unset.
#
# Use a fresh random value per deployment, e.g.
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"
# Changing it does not change any reported metric -- it only makes ledgers
# written under the old key unverifiable, so rotate between runs, never mid-run.
SIH26_LEDGER_KEY=replace-with-a-random-64-hex-string
